WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.748
Hi, I'm Strake!

00:00:02.748 --> 00:00:05.862
A small drafter guesses ahead, a big model checks.

00:00:05.862 --> 00:00:08.629
EAGLE-4 decides how far guessing is worth it.

00:00:10.183 --> 00:00:13.588
The idea: every extra guess has to earn its keep, against what

00:00:13.588 --> 00:00:15.859
it costs on this hardware, at this load.

00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:21.026
The drafter is one layer.

00:00:21.026 --> 00:00:23.534
It reads three hidden states from the big model.

00:00:23.954 --> 00:00:27.803
A cheap low-rank head shortlists a hundred and twenty-eight tokens, then

00:00:27.803 --> 00:00:29.203
scores only those exactly.

00:00:29.623 --> 00:00:32.099
It grows a tree of guesses, one depth at a time.

00:00:32.823 --> 00:00:37.102
After each depth, the harness asks: is one more level worth its measured cost?

00:00:37.522 --> 00:00:40.637
The best nodes go to the big model, which checks them in

00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:43.233
one pass and keeps the longest path it agrees with.

00:00:44.858 --> 00:00:45.982
Here is the formula.

00:00:46.858 --> 00:00:49.007
Gain is the extra tokens a guess should add.

00:00:49.516 --> 00:00:52.541
Cost is the extra time, measured once on this machine.

00:00:52.961 --> 00:00:54.992
The bar rises with depth and load.

00:00:54.992 --> 00:00:58.473
One user gets a deep tree; a busy server, a short chain.

00:00:59.858 --> 00:01:02.892
A block drafter pays for its whole block up front, even when

00:01:02.892 --> 00:01:04.915
the server is too busy to verify it.

00:01:05.336 --> 00:01:08.756
Drafting step by step decides before each guess, so a wasted

00:01:08.756 --> 00:01:10.000
guess is never made.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.835
The decode graph verifies sixteen rows, barely more than one normal step.

00:01:16.255 --> 00:01:19.646
Then five short draft depths, each launched straight from the GPU.

00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:26.019
Replaying coding agent sessions, one greedy user gets about five times the speed.

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:27.899
At sixteen users, under two.

00:01:29.225 --> 00:01:31.836
Remember: accepted tokens are half the story.

00:01:31.836 --> 00:01:33.702
EAGLE-4 prices the other half.

00:01:37.358 --> 00:01:40.865
A tiny drafter, a price on every guess, and the biggest win

00:01:40.865 --> 00:01:42.326
for one user writing code.

00:01:42.746 --> 00:01:44.821
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:44.821 --> 00:01:45.413
I'm Strake.

00:01:45.413 --> 00:01:45.710
Bye!
